course: religion-ethics
lesson: 57
title: Garden burials, wild animals and a new pet «too soon»
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A little burial in the garden is a way for people to say goodbye and thank you; the animal does not need it, but people often feel better afterwards, and it can have a prayer, a poem, a song or just a quiet moment.
explain: Picture Ollie and his dad under the apple tree with a small hole, a lolly-stick cross and Captain the goldfish; Ollie says, «Bye Captain, you were the best swimmer,» and that is enough.
explain: Wild animals act by instinct, not by right and wrong: the cat that leaves a mouse on the doormat and the fox that gets into the chicken coop are not wicked, they are being a cat and a fox, so we fix the fence rather than punish the fox.
explain: People grieve at different speeds: Tyler wants a new dog the next day, Chloe is furious when a new hamster arrives after nine days, and Ruby keeps her empty tank for months; a new pet never replaces the old one, and there is no timetable.
explain: Some things are better left alone: a dead animal on the road can carry germs, so we do not touch it, and a small grave is a place of rest, not something to dig up to look.
fact: A pet burial can have words, a song or silence; Christians might say a short prayer of thanks, and others a poem or a few words.
fact: A new pet does not replace the one that died; each is different and loved in its own way.
fact: Some people want a new pet soon and others need a long time; both are normal ways of grieving, and there is no religious rule about it.
fact: Hedgehogs often cross roads at night, and many are hit by cars; people make holes in garden fences to give them safer paths.
fact: Do not touch dead wild animals: they can carry germs; tell an adult if one is in a dangerous place.
fact: Baby birds are very hard to keep alive, even for experts; a fledgling on the ground is often still fed by its parents nearby.
fact: Cats are hunters by instinct; bringing prey home may be sharing or showing off, and a bell on the collar can warn birds and mice.
fact: After death a body slowly breaks down into the soil; digging up even a small grave is not a good idea and can spread germs.
fact: Ghost stories are made up; a dead pet does not come back to scratch or haunt anyone.
fact: Foxes hunt chickens because they are hungry; a stronger coop protects the hens better than anger.
fact: Christians and many other faiths treat graves as places of rest that should be left in peace.
fact: Jesus said God sees every sparrow that falls.
exercise: Ollie asks if they need to say words when burying Captain, like the vicar. What is true? | A few words, a song or silence are all fine; it is a goodbye | Only a vicar may say words at a burial | Without a long, proper prayer the fish can never rest in peace | Pets must never be given any goodbye at all
exercise: Chloe is furious when a new hamster arrives nine days after Nibbles died. Is her feeling fair? | Yes, some people need more time; a new pet doesn't replace the old one | No, she should be grateful at once | No, all hamsters are the same anyway | Yes, because nobody should ever have another pet
exercise: Tyler wants a new dog the day after his dog died. Is that heartless? | No, people grieve at different speeds; it isn't loving less | Yes, it shows he never loved his old dog | Yes, the church forbids anyone to get a new pet for a whole year | No, because dogs are all the same
exercise: Jade sees a squashed hedgehog on the road. What should she do? | Not touch it, and tell an adult if it is in a dangerous place | Pick it up carefully and take it home to bury it in the garden | Poke it with her foot to check it is dead | Take a photo and post it for a laugh
exercise: What probably happened to the hedgehog? | A car hit it as it crossed the road at night | It died of old age in the middle of the road | Another hedgehog pushed it into the road | It fell from a tree onto the road
exercise: Callum's baby bird died in the box overnight. Was it his fault? | No, baby birds are very hard to keep alive, even for experts | Yes, he should have fed it chips | Yes, boxes always kill birds | No, because the bird was already a ghost
exercise: Grace's cat leaves a dead mouse on the doormat. Why? | Cats hunt by instinct and may be sharing or showing off | The cat hates Grace and wants to upset her | The cat is evil and must be punished | The cat found it lying there and wants Grace to bury it properly
exercise: Nathan wants to dig up his goldfish to see what it looks like now. Good idea? | No, the body is breaking down and may carry germs; leave it in peace | Yes, every week, to check it is still there | Yes, to see if it has come back to life | No, because fish turn into gold under ground
exercise: Holly says Abbie's dead cat will come back as a ghost and scratch her. Is that true? | No, ghost stories are made up; a dead pet won't hurt anyone | Yes, cats always come back as ghosts | Yes, but only on Halloween | No, but dogs do come back as ghosts to scare the people they knew
exercise: A fox kills two of Lucas's chickens. What is the wise move? | Make the coop stronger; the fox was hungry, not wicked | Hunt the fox down and punish it | Get rid of all the other chickens | Leave the coop door open so the fox gets bored and won't come back
exercise: Ruby keeps her dead fish's empty tank for months. What should her dad do? | Let her decide in her own time | Throw the tank away while she is at school | Buy ten fish and fill it at once | Tell her she is being silly
exercise: What does a bell on a cat's collar do? | Warns birds and mice that the cat is coming | Stops the cat from ever hunting anything again | Makes the cat able to talk to its owners at night | Tells the owner when the cat is hungry for dinner
exercise: How do Christians and many faiths see graves? | As places of rest, to be left in peace | As places to dig in for buried treasure | As places for children to play games in | As places that do not really matter at all
exercise: Why do people make small holes in garden fences? | To give hedgehogs safer paths between gardens | To let cats get through and hunt much more easily | To let rain drain out of gardens | To feed foxes at night
test: Ollie's mate from Newcastle says, «Howay, just chuck the fish in the bin, man.» («Howay» means «come on».) What is a kinder choice? | A small burial with a few words, as a goodbye | Throw him in the bin with the rest of the rubbish | Keep him in the freezer for later, just in case | Leave him floating in the tank for another week
test: Chloe screams, «You're replacing him!» when Mum brings home a new hamster. What might put it right? | Mum says sorry for rushing, and Chloe chooses a name when ready | Mum takes all pets away for good | Chloe must love the new hamster today | Mum names it Nibbles so nothing changes
test: Jade's mate yells, «Ew ew EW, that's rank!» at the dead hedgehog. («Rank» means «disgusting».) What is the best response? | Feeling sick and sad is normal; don't touch it | Pick it up to show the class | Kick it into the bushes | Laugh about it all day at school with everyone else
test: What would happen if Callum had left the baby bird where it was? | Its parents might have kept feeding it nearby | It would certainly have turned into a little frog | It would have flown off at once all the way to Africa | Nothing at all would ever have happened to it after that
test: Callum mutters, «I tried to save it and it still died. I hate it.» What is true and kind? | Trying to help was good, even though it didn't work | He killed it himself by trying far too hard to help it | He should never try to help any wild animals ever again | The bird died just because he hates things so very much
test: A Gen Alpha kid says the cat's dead mouse «is giving villain, fr». («It's giving» means «it looks like»; «fr» means «for real».) What is true? | The cat is hunting by instinct, not doing wrong like a person | The cat is a real villain on purpose | The cat should go to prison | The mouse was killed by a person
test: Holly giggles, «Tigger's gonna haunt you, lol.» Abbie is scared. What could Abbie say? | «That's made up and it wasn't funny» | «You're right, I'll never sleep again» | «Ghosts only haunt people who loved their pets» | «I'll buy a ghost trap tomorrow»
test: Lucas shouts, «I'm gonna kill that fox!» What is the wise answer? | Anger is normal, but fix the fence, not the fox | Yes, go out and kill every single fox in the county | Get a pet fox instead, so it guards the chickens | Blame the chickens for being far too slow to escape
test: A Canadian uncle says, «Eh, buddy, just grab another fish at the store, it's no big deal.» Ruby says «not yet, maybe never». Who is right? | Ruby may wait as long as she needs; there is no timetable | The uncle, because all fish are the same, so it's no big deal | Ruby must buy one by Sunday | Nobody may ever keep a fish again
test: A cartoon shows a boy digging up his goldfish, who pops out alive and waves. What is wrong? | The fish is dead and its body is breaking down; it won't come back | Fish really do come back if you dig | Goldfish can live under ground | The boy should dig deeper to find it
test: Tyler's mum says, «A new dog tomorrow is heartless.» Tyler says the house is too quiet. What is fair? | Both feelings are normal; talk about it as a family | Mum is right, it shows he never really loved his dog at all | Tyler is right, and grief is silly | Neither, because dogs don't matter
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the purpose of a domestic burial of a deceased pet? | To afford the family an occasion for farewell and gratitude | To satisfy a legal requirement imposed upon all owners of animals | To permit the animal's subsequent return to life | To fulfil an obligation upon the animal itself
exam: Identify the course of action most consistent with prudence upon encountering a deceased wild animal upon the highway. | Refraining from contact and informing an adult where necessary | Removing the carcass by hand to a domestic garden | Examining the carcass closely for amusement | Disposing of the carcass in a household refuse bin by hand
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning predation by the domestic cat. | It proceeds from instinct and not from moral wickedness | It constitutes a deliberate act of malice against its owner | It is learnt exclusively from observation of humans | It is a symptom of illness requiring treatment
exam: Ascertain the appropriate interval between the death of a pet and the acquisition of another. | No fixed interval exists; it differs from person to person | Precisely one calendar year in every instance | Not less than nine days, as is required by long-standing custom | It is required to occur on the following day
